                "quoted_status_profile_username": "ChristinaPushaw",
                "reply_count": 165,
                "retweet_count": 227,
                "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
                "text": "This entire essay is a shocking admission.  \n\nThe core argument Lawson is making is that we are LUCKY the now-Attorney General used his power as then-chief-of-staff to syphon money away from Florida\u2019s medicaid program to fund political ads against a marijuana amendment (which, by the way, I publicly opposed.)\n\nThey aren\u2019t saying: \u201cJames didn\u2019t organize a scheme to launder $10 million from a managed care provider to his PAC to fund ant-weed ads\u201d\n\nInstead they\u2019re saying: \u201cIt\u2019s a good thing he did!\u201d\n\nSo here is my question - what is the limiting principle?  Can anyone with the power of the State in a legal dispute dictate to a settling party what private entities must receive money to ultimately get used in political ads?  If so, that won\u2019t end well for conservatives.\n\nIf \u201cI ordered the code red\u201d is really the @JamesUthmeierFL argument here, the Florida House is likely to consider impeaching the Attorney General.",
